('H', '1. What it is'),
('P', 'Schizotypal personality disorder (STPD) is a long-standing pattern of **social and interpersonal deficits** (acute discomfort with close relationships) combined with **odd thinking, perceptions, and behavior**, beginning by early adulthood (American Psychiatric Association [APA], 2022). It belongs to **Cluster A** and is also listed in DSM-5-TR’s schizophrenia spectrum chapter, because it shares many genetic, biological, and thinking features with schizophrenia (Koenigsberg et al., 2003; Rosell et al., 2014).'),
('P', '**How common:** in a U.S. national survey, 3.9% of adults met lifetime criteria (4.2% of men, 3.7% of women). STPD was linked to substantial mental disability in both sexes and frequently co-occurred with mood, anxiety, and other personality disorders (Pulay et al., 2009).'),
('P', '**Counselor framing:** these clients are often lonely, anxious, and misunderstood. Their unusual ideas are usually held with some doubt, not with the fixed certainty of a delusion, and their social anxiety tends not to ease with familiarity.'),
